#passage(("Every church father had his own conception of the trinity, by the way. There's a long list of what they call heresies. So it's not that you, the poor person, cannot understand the trinity; even the church fathers are like all having their own ideas of the trinity. We have a council 325, nearly 400 years after Jesus, of [Arius], who was a church father, arguing with Athanasius, or another church father, about the divinity of Jesus: was he a lesser god, with the idea of subordination and arianism, all of that. And then you have 381: they didn't know what the holy spirit is. No, it's not you cannot understand; it's that none of them understood.", "St. Augustine, that prolific church father, has this entire story about the beach and how he tries to conceptualize the trinity, and then in the end he says it's a mystery. When the Christians say it's a mystery, they got it from St. Augustine. They got it from the church fathers. It's a mystery.", "If we can understand the trinity, then bring me one explanation, anybody who explain the [trinity], and I'm more than happy to listen. Shouldn't something that God revealed be for everyone anyways? Even if I agree with you that only a select few people can understand it, shouldn't the revelation of God, God telling us about himself, be conceivable to everybody? Or if we reject, then how can God blame us because we couldn't understand?",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Lady In Total Shock After Asking A Muslim Why He's Not Christian! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cvMC08Nnc-o", ((184, "3:04–3:36"), (219, "3:39–3:52"), (234, "3:54–4:27"),), id: 3863)
