#passage(("When Jesus is praying, who is he praying to? Is he praying to the Trinity or just the father? He's just praying to the father. So how can they be equal?", "According to the Bible, Mark's gospel, chapter 13:32, Jesus says no one knows the day and the hour, not the angels in heaven, not the son, only the father. So not even the holy spirit. So how can they be equal when one knows something and the other doesn't?", "The Bible is written in language and language conveys meaning. People can twist what it says, but there are explicit statements there. I'll give you an example. Does God have a God that he worships? God is the god of everyone, but Jesus said he has a God. You're supposed to be equal. It's not supposed to be another God; supposed to be another person in the same God. But he said to Mary Magdalene: I'm going back to my father and your father, my God and your God.", "In John 17:3 he said the only true God is the father, not the holy Spirit, not the son. He said the Father, which is one person, which is not the son and not the holy spirit. So if he literally explicitly says to you the only true God is the father, how can you claim he's God? Because he took away the sins of the world? But that doesn't mean you're God. Even if I accept that to be true, God can use someone to take away the sins of the world. Doesn't mean he's God.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “3 Christian Students Encounter A Muslim! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n6o1Z_t3EQ0", ((58, "0:58–1:21"), (214, "3:34–3:52"), (257, "4:17–4:48"), (292, "4:52–5:18"),), id: 4043)
