#passage(("The Jews didn't understand this in the same way that Avery did. They actually assumed that Jesus was calling out to Elijah, a previous Prophet. They didn't think that Jesus was calling out to the Creator; they assumed that Jesus was calling out to the creation.", "So let's break this down. According to [Avery's] Theory, he's saying that Jesus was quoting Psalms 22 verse1, because 15 verses later there is a verse that speaks of the crucifixion, which of course is completely inaccurate. If Jesus wanted to quote something to do with the crucifixion, he should have quoted the verse directly instead of quoting a verse 15 verses before it. Imagine Jesus while on the cross saying \"I am a worm, not a man\". Would this be evidence of the crucifixion? But instead what you're saying is, what we have here is God on the cross calling out to God, asking God \"why did you forsake God\", but he actually meant to say \"I am the fulfillment of the prophecy\".", "Now let's just take this at face value. I'm not going to provide you with some sort of Islamic interpretation. I'm just going to read the text for what it is. Here is what it has to say. This occurred a day before the [crucifixion] of Jesus in The Garden of Gethsemane: \"They went to a place called Gethsemane, and Jesus said to his disciples, 'Sit here while I pray.' He took Peter, James and John along with him, and he began to be deeply distressed and troubled. 'My soul is overwhelmed with sorrow to the point of death,' he said to them. 'Stay here and keep watch.' Going a little farther, he fell to the ground and prayed that if possible the hour might pass from him. 'Abba, Father,' he said, 'everything is possible for you. Take this cup from me. Yet not what I will, but what you will.'\"", "So as we can see, Jesus is fully aware of what's about to happen. He's aware that he's going to be betrayed, he's aware that he's going to be killed, it's going to be a very painful death, and he's displaying fear, and he wants God to save him. He wants God to take this cup away from him. He falls to the ground, he's praying; instead he's crucified, and while on the cross he says \"my God, my God, why have you forsaken me?\"", "Now naturally this doesn't make much sense for someone that believes in the Divinity of Jesus, because naturally God isn't supposed to say something to God. However, if you open yourself up to the text, you will realize that Jesus himself is a broken Jesus, and he's screaming out these words due to the pain that he's actually in, and it's in direct contradiction with this idea, this suggestion that he is God. It isn't some sort of hidden message that the people there at the time didn't get. If you're going to quote scripture in order to get your feelings across, you don't quote scripture that completely opposes the reality of your situation, saying \"my God, my God, why have you forsaken me\" when you actually meant \"hey, I know that I'm going to be saved and I'm going to conquer death\". That doesn't make any sense.", "So me as a Muslim, this doesn't mean much to me. I don't believe in the Bible, I don't believe in the gospels as authoritative, I don't accept the reports of non-eyewitnesses. But if you are a Christian and you believe that these words are inspired, and you believe in the authority and the preservation of the Gospel of Mark and the gospel of Matthew, then you should be rejecting the Divinity of Jesus based on the last words uttered by him.",), "Farid Responds, “Godlogic Explains \"Why have you Forsaken me?!\"”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9Wsz9OAVeqY", ((338, "5:38–5:54"), (354, "5:54–6:59"), (419, "6:59–8:00"), (480, "8:00–8:26"), (509, "8:29–9:30"), (570, "9:30–10:06"),), id: 4046)
