#passage(("I'm giving you these examples why the trinity is not applicable to the Bible, from the Bible. I'll give you another example. It's a very common well-known verse. You say to me that they're all coequal, co-eternal. They all have the attributes of God. So, Jesus had the attributes of God when he was walking on earth, which means Jesus was all powerful. He was all knowing. So, if you say that Jesus had the [attributes] of God, he's all powerful, all knowing, eternal, all of these attributes of God, he possessed them, then if Jesus say he doesn't know something, then that means your belief about Jesus is false. He's not equal with the father. He's not equal with the Holy Spirit. He said he doesn't know the hour.", "You're about to tell me he did not reveal the hour, not know the hour. The problem with this lousy argument is that the word used in Greek is used for to know, not to reveal. And that word is used in multiple places in the Bible to mean to know, not to reveal. You cannot do that with this Greek word, because this Greek word means to know.", "I'm telling you why these arguments are lousy: when you go, for example, to church fathers, many church fathers, and you look at their explanations of this verse, they try to explain how Jesus doesn't know. Even the church fathers who read Greek, who are Greek, many of them themselves Greek, understanding literature more than any Christian today, they are understanding the verse to mean to know. They took it from one church father talking about reveal or not revealed, but many church fathers talked about the idea of knowing and Jesus not knowing, and they try to reconcile it. That's why I call it lousy, because if they were to go to their own church fathers, who are interpreting these verses and explaining them, they would be stuck.", "The Greek says to know, and it's present in many many places in the New Testament. If Jesus wanted to say reveal, God is not the author of confusion. God is not going to say [know] instead of reveal, because people are going to get confused. If God wanted to say to the people reveal, he's not going to go to the people and say I don't know something. Like humans are going to reveal the hour or something? Makes no sense in context. He says no one knows the day and the hour, not the angels in heaven, not the son, only the father.", "This is a lie, because he didn't say reveal. Don't claim he said something he didn't say. You have people who are trying to do this patchup work and say [know] means reveal. I don't care what you think it means. The word used here is to know, not to reveal.", "This is Mark 13:32. Here's the word. It occurs 22 times. Matthew 6:8: Father knoweth what things. Matthew 6:32: knows. Matthew 24:36: knows. Mark 4:27: knows. Mark [13:32]: knows. Luke 12:30: [knows]. John 7:15: knows. Here is the lexicon. This Greek here, strong Greek, occurs these amount of times, and it tells you the word means to know. It's used in all of these different instances in the Bible to mean know. Even in Mark 13:32, go to all the translations. They all say [know].", "All you need is two minute research. I'm going to go and click Greek Bible and go to the word used all over the Bible: it's used all over the Bible to mean [know], but all of a sudden here, I have a problem that it's not going to mean [know]; I'm going to change what the Greek actually means. We got to be honest. That's why I'm telling you these arguments don't work when we go into the nitty-gritty of things. You can't play, we can't play this game with the scripture. Scripture is clear. Jesus says he doesn't know. That's it. It's clear. It's simple.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Passionate Christian Argues For The Trinity With A Muslim! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ntsF1iRj8IM", ((2115, "35:15–36:07"), (2182, "36:22–36:56"), (2229, "37:09–37:52"), (2279, "37:59–38:33"), (2327, "38:47–39:03"), (2391, "39:51–40:43"), (2448, "40:48–41:30"),), id: 4051)
