#passage(("What we say as Muslims is that Allah, The Creator of the Heavens and the Earth, is not a man or a woman. He doesn't die. He doesn't come on Earth, because when you come on Earth, you eat and you drink and there's a lot of things that you do after that, which is not befitting God. God is a Holy Creator, so He's not subjugated by these human things, He's not limited by these things, He's independent.", "When you read the Bible, you will see that Jesus said to worship The Creator alone. He used the term \"Father\". We don't use the term \"Father\", because we say the term \"Father\" caused the confusion of the Trinity: because when people start to believe in \"Father\", then he has a Son, then he has the Holy Spirit. So in Islam, we believe Allahﷻ is The Creator, we are the Creation. And when the Jews used to use the word \"son\" or \"Father\", it usually means: when you meet someone in the street, some old man says \"my son\" or \"my daughter\", he doesn't mean you're literally his son, biological son; it's a metaphorical thing, which means that God takes care of you. He looks after you like your father looks after you. The Creator looks after you, He takes care of you, He provides for you. This concept of father and son comes from this part: that someone takes care of you. But it does not literally mean that God became pregnant and then He gave birth or something, because God doesn't become pregnant.", "What we say as Muslims, we say Jesus was born miraculously. And this is very interesting. If I say that Jesus has a father, doesn't that mean that I'm denying the miraculous birth? Because the miraculous birth says he has no father. So how can I say he was born miraculously and in the same time he has a father? That's why Islam makes sense: because in Islam we believe in the Virgin Birth, but we don't believe Jesus has a father. Creator is not the Father of human beings. He was born miraculously, which makes it a miracle, but he has no father.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “French Girls Interested In Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rl6DIJLkZu8", ((447, "7:27–7:42"), (472, "7:52–8:37"), (528, "8:48–9:29"),), id: 4253)
