#passage(("Allah says in the Quran, in the verse (alimran3:49): وَرَسُولًا إِلَىٰ بَنِىٓ إِسْرَٰٓءِيلَ (And a Messenger). So the verse is saying a Messenger: not God, not God incarnate, not a part of a Trinity. The verse is explicitly saying that Jesus is the Messenger from God. That's it. And the Quran explicitly anyway says (alMaidah5:75) مَّا ٱلْمَسِيحُ ٱبْنُ مَرْيَمَ إِلَّا رَسُولٌۭ (Jesus is nothing except a Messenger), explicitly mentioned in the Quran. So nothing more, and nothing less, because Messengers are higher than human beings, normal human beings.", "So Allah first says that Jesus is a Messenger: وَرَسُولًا إِلَىٰ بَنِىٓ إِسْرَٰٓءِيلَ أَنِّى قَدْ جِئْتُكُم بِـَٔايَةٍۢ مِّن رَّبِّكُمْ (I came to you with a sign) from ME? No! That's not what the verse is saying. The verse is saying (and I came to you with a sign from YOUR LORD). Just like what Jesus says in the Bible, \"I by myself can do nothing\", in John5:30. He cannot do anything by himself. And just like (Acts2:22) says, Jesus of Nazareth was A MAN, just a man, appointed by God, by miracles, wonders, and signs, which GOD DID through him. God is The One Who did the miracles. Allah is The One here. He's doing the miracle; according to Jesus' words himself, he's saying (I brought you a sign from your Lord), not from me! (I brought you a sign from Allah), not from me. \"Your Lord\": a sign from your God, Allah.", "جِئْتُكُم بِـَٔايَةٍۢ مِّن رَّبِّكُمْ ۖ أَنِّىٓ أَخْلُقُ لَكُم مِّنَ ٱلطِّينِ كَهَيْـَٔةِ ٱلطَّيْرِ. So what he says: (I fashion, from clay, the shape of a bird). So when he says create here, he's talking about fashioning from clay a shape of a bird. He's not talking about actual making and creation of human beings. Now, the word \"create\" here is used in the Quran in many instances, and it does NOT mean the Creation that is only for God. So the Quran (al'Ankabut29:17), for instance, would use the word: وَتَخْلُقُونَ إِفْكًا (and you create lies). When we say \"only Allah creates\", we're talking about a very specific type of creation. We're not talking about this type of creation.", "So the creation in our belief is divided into two things. You either have creation which is changing one thing from one form to another, or using a previous thing that exists and changing it into a new form. So that's one type of creation that even humans are able to do. Like the person who created this bottle: when you say person created this bottle, by creating the bottle, it means that they fashioned plastic from one shape to another shape. Or creating a chair, or creating a house, all of these \"creating\", the word خَلْق in the Arabic language: that's one way to look at it, which both humans and God can do, changing from one substance to another. Now, the Creation that is ONLY for Allah is to create without the need of a prior substance, or another substance in existence, or changing one thing from one form to another. So that Creation that is only for Allah is to create by just saying \"BE\" and it is: what we would say from nothing, without the need of any prior substance, without the need of the prior existence of anything.", "So when he says here \"create\", he's talking about fashioning, shaping one thing from one to another. And then he says فَأَنفُخُ فِيهِ (then I blow in that clay-looking like a bird) فَيَكُونُ طَيْرًۢا بِإِذْنِ ٱللَّهِ (Then it becomes a real bird with the permission of God). So how does it become a real bird? With Jesus' creation? No! The creation of Jesus is shaping it into looking like a bird. That's it. That's the creation part. The fashioning, changing it from one substance to another, that is the creation part, which any human being can do. Now the bird becoming an actual living bird, بِإِذْنِ اللهِ (with the permission of God): God is The One Who does it! Just like the verse says (I came to you with a sign from your Lord).",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| The Quran Proves Jesus Is God?! Sunnis Don't Pray Like The Prophet?! | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qbnzQHBdTJo", ((358, "5:58–6:33"), (405, "6:45–7:28"), (452, "7:32–8:15"), (498, "8:18–9:15"), (558, "9:18–9:56"),), id: 4266)
