#passage(("He mentions the crucifixion, and he mentions it as a criticism of Islam, which is not. What he mentions here is that the Quran says \"Wa qawlihim, and their saying, inna qatalna al-Masiha Isa ibn Maryam rasul Allah.\" We killed Jesus the Messiah, the messenger of God. So, he's saying: oh, they wouldn't kill him if they believed he was the Messiah, because the verse says the Messiah, the son of Mary. We always tell them the one who's describing him with the title the Messiah, the son of Mary is Allah. Allah is saying that they said \"Wa qawlihim\", their statement. They made a statement that they killed Jesus. Allah is the one giving him this title. Allah is not saying that they believe that he is the Messiah and he's the messenger of God, because of course, if they did believe he was the Messiah and if they did believe that he was the messenger of God, they wouldn't say it.", "Even if we take his understanding, we don't have an issue. If we were to take this understanding and say that they are calling him the Messiah: they can call him the Messiah and mocking him. And what's really hilarious is you have in Matthew where the Jewish people, at the alleged crucifixion in the Bible, they're saying \"Hail, the king of the Jews.\" They're mocking him on the cross by calling him the king of the Jews, which is what the Messiah is. So, when they're calling him Messiah there, it's not that they believe he's the king of the Jews, but they're mocking him. So, they can say the Messiah as a mockery, not necessarily meaning that they actually truly believe it. So, there's no really issues with this idea of what the Quran says.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| Wess Huff Claims The Quran Was Copied From The Bible | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hb-kHiXbGxI", ((407, "6:47–7:48"), (471, "7:51–8:32"),), id: 4308)
