#passage(("The Quran doesn't say Allah made it appear so. It's the Quran chapter 4 verse 157. And their boasting: we killed Jesus the Messiah, but they killed him not and they crucified him not. But it was made to appear so. Allah doesn't say I made it to appear so. I highlight the Arabic word: Shubbiha. It was made to appear so. It doesn't say shabbahtu, I made it to appear so. So the first point you made, about Allah deceiving people, is false, because Allah doesn't say he's the one who made it appear so.", "Now your question is: who made it? The answer I will give you is I don't know. The verse itself says the people who differed about Jesus are in doubt about it. They have no knowledge about it. So, I'm making the claim I do not know the exact person who made it appear so. And I can say confidently I do not know. But what I can say is that you misrepresent the Quran if you say that Allah is the one who did, because Allah didn't say it.", "Now, the second part is about deceiving. Where is the deception that is in the verse? Somebody made it to appear so and deceived the people. Now, you say why Allah didn't stop them. If they believed it, where is the problem? Why does Allah need to stop it? Deception is done with an evil intent for an evil outcome. It is showing something opposite to its reality with an evil intent for an evil outcome. So, I'll take your own definition. So, deceiving does not need to be evil. So, the deceiving in that verse is not evil. You need to show me the bad in it. I'm claiming there's no bad. If the people believe that Jesus died on the cross but they did not believe in the Trinity and they did not believe he was God, where is the evil in that, in a Muslim perspective?", "I'm not saying it's okay to deceive anybody. I told you already that it's not Allah who deceived. If I say somebody deceived the people to think Jesus was crucified, let's accept that. I don't have a problem. We do not have an issue with this specific thing if some rando