as well. Hence the contradiction in the reporting of the story, hence the untrustworthiness of these authors.", "Jesus did not correct him. He said that God is saying this about you, and Jesus affirmed that. He went with that narrative. Jesus affirmed that narrative, and the Christian scholars believe in that. Christians in generally believe that Psalms 91 is about Jesus. But they don't read it. They just don't read it. If you read it, you'll be like, okay, uh-oh, we have an issue here.", "Whoever the author of Matthew is, this was his methodology. He would look at any statement that Jesus made and then he was going to look in the Old Testament. If Jesus says I like potatoes, he found a statement in the Old Testament says I like potatoes. So: oh look, it's a prophecy about Jesus, because the same statement here is the same statement there. This is his methodology when he talked about Jesus. And the issue now you start seeing is: all of these prophecies, when you start looking at them, you start investigating them, you see all of them talking about protection. And this is exactly the Islamic narrative: that Allah protected Jesus, saved him, rescued him, as the Psalms 91 is saying. And Allah raised him up, like the verses are saying. Delivered him, gave him safety, salvation, he raised him up and nobody crucified him. So it agrees with the Islamic description here of the events that would happen to Jesus.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Skeptical Christian Questions The Quranic Narrative Of The Crucifixion! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SNOcgXxTrxQ", ((1869, "31:09–32:06"), (1941, "32:21–33:23"), (2006, "33:26–33:55"), (2064, "34:24–35:11"), (2116, "35:16–36:01"), (2163, "36:03–36:31"), (2207, "36:47–37:06"), (2232, "37:12–38:03"),), id: 4339)
