#passage(("How many of these people actually saw him come back to life? At the end of the day, you only have two eyewitnesses. Well, that's if we're being generous. I'm of course referring to Matthew and John. And by the way, in reality, those two authors are anonymous. Now, Yong Hun, being the holder of the highest IQ in the world, is very aware that two people making a claim is insufficient. So, he goes on and he says, \"Well, many people are claiming this, and many people have claimed to see him alive.\" If I said that a thousand people saw Young Hoon flying, that's just one person making the claim. That's just one testimony. It's not a thousand testimonies, because it's just me that's saying it.", "Yong Hun said that his followers were willing to die for the belief that Jesus was resurrected. And this is incorrect and it cannot be proven. There's not a single example of a disciple of Christ or of a follower of Christ dying specifically because of the resurrection.",), "Farid Responds, “276 IQ Christian vs Average Muslim Bro”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CphY63Xvjbg", ((200, "3:20–4:23"), (283, "4:43–5:03"),), id: 4361)
// U FR073:37-61 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("In Acts 5, the apostles are put in jail and flogged before an angel allegedly opened the door to the cell. And the reason they were arrested was because the high priest was jealous of their faith healing and the miracles and whatnot. Nothing about the resurrection here. So David's conversion to Christianity, the way he portrays it, it seemed as if the disciples had swords to their necks and they were told to denounce their faith in the resurrection and they said, \"No, we shall not deny the resurrection.\" And it was due to their strong belief in that David had to concede that they indeed witnessed the resurrection. But now, 28 years later, it seems like David has watered down the initial claim. Now, it's Peter and John spent a night in jail. Do you realize how underwhelming that is and how unconvincing that is as a proof for the resurrection? Imagine them being in jail and then the warden walks up to them and says, \"Okay, guys, no more preaching the resurrection.\" So they say, \"Nah, we're going to continue preaching the resurrection.\" And then the warden says, \"Okay, you can leave.\"", "What I was expecting from the video was evidence, proof of references that all 12, or not all 12 because Judas betrayed Jesus. But remember, David said most of the disciples were killed due to the resurrection. And instead we got this, which is proof that David no longer believes in this, because if you believed in that, then you'd be providing your strongest evidence, not Peter and John spent a night in jail. But David actually does present an example of one disciple that was killed in Acts 12. James the brother of John is put to death by Herod, and Peter is again put in jail. But it doesn't really say why he was killed. But let's assume that James did believe in the resurrection and that that was the reason that he was put to death. That still wouldn't work, because, according to your criteria, James testimony alone isn't sufficient.",), "Farid Responds, “David Wood Realizes Resurrection Can't be Proven!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hi_wn5x0p1U", ((208, "3:28–4:42"), (284, "4:44–5:51"),), id: 4362)
// U FR037:21-97 | Farid Responds | 6
#passage(("The apostles were martyred, therefore, they believed in the resurrection, therefore, Jesus rose from the dead. That's the gist of the argument that Christians base their faith on. Now, Sean's work in this specific topic did damage this argument, and credit should be given to him for being fair with the historical accounts. Well, at least more fair than most Christians. And, of course, a lot of Christians are not happy with these findings.", "Now, I don't want to dwell too much in regards to the criticisms against Foxe's Book of Martyrs. However, it's a book that was written about 450 years ago, and it conflicts with a lot of the information that you find in earlier sources from the 2nd and 3rd century. And, in fact, there's some details within this book that can only be seen as fabrication. This would actually be red flags if you're reading Foxe's Book of Martyrs, because Foxe's Book of Martyrs is listing dates, saying Philip died in '54 and this and that. I don't know who came up with that.", "Now, Sean categorizes the martyrdoms of the apostles from highest probability to the lowest, which is very unlikely, and he concludes that there are only two from among the disciples that were at the highest level. And after David listens to Sean's presentation and he takes the information in, he's convinced by what Sean has to say. And he beseeches other Christians to drop these bad arguments.", "Now, there are a couple of criticisms that I'd like to make towards Sean's video and some of his work. Despite him bringing the number down from 12 to two, I feel that there's some issues with some of his conclusions. Because even those that he is quite fairly certain that they were martyred, like Peter, the information that he has provided is lacking. So, the first issue is Sean will quote sources that are not explicit at all about Peter being martyred. And then he will quote sources that mention a martyrdom, but will have nothing to do with the resurrection.", "I'm granting for the sake of the discussion that the Gospel of John is authentically attributed to John. But when we look at the quote, we find nothing explicit about Peter being killed. What we find isn't even a report about Peter's death, but a prophecy by Jesus, which, again, isn't explicit at all. As for the quote from Clement, if we do accept these words as indicative of a martyrdom, we find that this didn't occur because of him believing in the resurrection, but rather because of jealousy. According to another source provided by Sean, which is the Acts of Peter, the jealousy may have been tied to Agrippa and Albinus, since their wives and concubines didn't want to have intercourse with them after their conversion to Christianity. Now, regardless as to what is meant here, we're jumping from jealousy being the cause for Peter's martyrdom all the way to Peter was martyred because of the resurrection, his belief in it, and his call towards the deity of Christ. That's quite a leap. And after all, the Romans would kill you for much less than claiming that Christ is a deity, as Sean admits.", "I'm sincerely appreciative of what Sean has done, especially when you compare it to David's initial cause for conversion. Anyhow, the challenge: prove that the disciples were martyred because of their claims about the resurrection. Not because of jealousy, not because of any other random reason. And you see, I can clearly tell that there is a lacking of evidence. And, of course, there were Christians early on that did not believe the resurrection, did not even believe in the crucifixion. In any case, this can't even be proven with Peter, who is at the top of the list, according to Sean.",), "Farid Responds, “Nobody died for the Resurrection of Jesus”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3ZN9rxNQMTQ", ((123, "2:03–2:32"), (203, "3:23–3:59"), (239, "3:59–4:29"), (294, "4:54–5:35"), (366, "6:06–7:19"), (495, "8:15–9:33"),), id: 4363)

